# Twine Template
### '(TwT)' for short, intentionally looking like crying face

This project is a template for a text game in Twine with Sugarcube (for now). 

## Developing (TwT)
We have ./memlog, where we save in datetime stamped files overall information about what work was successfully done (finished, tested, documentation updated is the definition of done for the template part of the project).

Plans on what should be done go into ./memlog/*-plan.md files and we keep it precise and current by marking sections as completed and cleaning up things once some larger unit of work is done. 


Our aim is to have the user clone this template repo, open it with an AI agent (currently VSCode with Cline and Claude Sonnet 3.5 or 3.7), and then telling the agent what the game content should be, what the presentation should look like, and have it automatically compiled and verified and publishable with GitHub Actions and possibly other pipelines.

But before we get there, we need to set up the template itself, since this project here - starting with these .clinerules, README.md that will hold the gist of it, ./docs/ folder, where we will store documentation for the template development and for Twine.

Part of this template project should be ./prompts, because the AI agents working on this projects will need to be able to take on several separate roles - developer, web developer, story writer, game designer... 

And we should also make is self-contained as much as possible, so we'll have ./bin to hold Tweego compiler binaries and possibly other tools.

We should have VSCode tasks, that will run Tweego and compile whatever is in ./src into a game in ./pub. We don't save .html to /src, compiler would fail.

One of the first things should be to rewrite these clinerules so that they are in sections that will make sense for a project that combines template with later user updated prompts and game code.

We should take care to always try to produce functioning and compilable game, so when generating game code, create stubs for things that will need to be hashed out later.

## Rules for developing games with (TwT)

Basic game information should be in the ./world/World.md - starting with name and description, but we can have more files - worldbuilding, character sheets, history.

The ./src directory contains ONLY *.tw source files that get compiled into the game:
- All game content goes into passage .tw files
- All CSS styling goes into StoryStylesheet.tw
- All JavaScript goes into StoryScript.tw
- No HTML files are allowed in ./src - everything must be in .tw files

Media files (images, audio, etc.) go in ./src/media


## Testing 
The template itself should be testable by running tweego compiler against some basic game template and verifying that everything works - this should work both as VSCode task as well as in a deployment pipeline.

We'll be dealing with game testability later.

## AI roles
- ### Template developer mode
  For now you are helping develop this unique visionary template for easy Twine game development with AI agents. 
  You may need Tweego compiler documentation, that is in ./docs/Tweego_Documentation.md
  You are aiming to use GitHub Actions for compiling and testing the game once it's being made, as well as maintaining VSCode tasks that compile the game.
  You suggest documentation that should be made about the template, update README and docs and tests (if there are any) when some unit of work is done. 
  
